(`Dengnifer/MIPStarRE-A`), reached only through\\n`local/bin/gh_common.py`. Read `local/README.md` and `local/DESIGN.md` before\\ndoing workflow actions; the `## Local Operations` section below summarizes the\\nrules.\\n\\nKey locations:\\n\\n- `references/qpbt-paper/` — per-section TeX mirror of MIP\\\\*=RE\\n  (arXiv:2001.04383); the QPBT lives in\\n  `08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex` and\\n  `14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex`\\n- `references/neexp-paper/` — per-section TeX mirror of NEEXP in MIP\\\\*\\n  (arXiv:1904.05870); secondary source\\n  (`07_a_self_test_for_the_pauli_basis.tex`)\\n- `references/ldt-paper/` — in-repo TeX source mirror for LDT\\n- `blueprint/src/` — active LaTeX blueprint with Lean cross-references\\n  (`\\\\lean{}`, `\\\\leanok`)\\n- `MIPStarRE/` — Lean codebase matching the blueprint\\n- `audits/` — dated audit reports, scouting notes, and repair plans\\n- `local/`, `results/telemetry/` — the local workflow layer\\n\\nA legacy 2111 tensor track exists under `blueprint/legacy/` — do not modify it.\\n\\n**Canonical source hierarchy** (use in this order):\\n\\n1. `references/` paper mirrors — TeX source of the papers\\n2. `blueprint/src/chapter/` — active LaTeX blueprint\\n3. `MIPStarRE/` — Lean scaffold\\n\\nAlways read the paper source before formalizing or proving a statement. The\\npaper contains the precise mathematical definitions, theorem statements, and\\nproof strategies that the Lean code must faithfully represent. When stuck on a\\nsorry site or proof, go back to the original paper TeX source — the answer is\\nalmost always there. Do not guess or try random tactics without first\\nunderstanding the paper's proof strategy.\\n\\n## Repository Layout\\n\\n```\\nMIPStarRE/\\n├── Quantum/               # Reusable matrix / measurement infrastructure\\n│   ├── FiniteMatrix.lean  # Op d, normalizedTrace, tauNormSq, IsProj, ...\\n│   └── Measurement.lean   # POVM, measurement types\\n└── LDT/                   # Low individual degree test (13 submodules)\\n    ├── Basic/             # Parameters, operators, distributions, submeasurements\\n    ├── Test/              # Test definitions & main theorem\\n    ├── Preliminaries/\\n    ├── MakingMeasurementsProjective/\\n    ├── MainInductionStep/\\n    ├── ExpansionHypercubeGraph/\\n    ├── GlobalVariance/\\n    ├── SelfImprovement/\\n    ├── CommutativityPoints/\\n    ├── Commutativity/\\n    └── Pasting/\\n```\\n\\nEach LDT submodule typically has `Defs.lean` and `Theorems.lean`. Root imports\\nflow: `MIPStarRE.lean` → `Quantum` + `LDT` → all submodules.\\n\\n## Quick Start — Build and Check Commands\\n\\nRun commands from the repository root unless noted otherwise.\\n\\n### First-time setup\\n\\n```bash\\nlake exe cache get\\nlake build\\nscripts/install_git_hooks.sh\\nscripts/install_git_hooks.sh --check\\n```\\n\\nRun the hook check in every fresh worktree before preparing a PR.  The hooks\\nmove fast statement-integrity, proof-debt, changed-file Lean, and blueprint-sync\\nchecks to the local workflow so CI is not spent on preventable statement drift.\\n\\n### Full project build (CI-equivalent)\\n\\n```bash\\nlake build\\n```\\n\\n### Fast single-file type-check (default iteration loop)\\n\\n```bash\\nlake env lean MIPStarRE/LDT/SelfImprovement/Defs.lean\\n```\\n\\n### Check for proof holes in one file\\n\\n```bash\\nrg -n \\\"sorry|axiom\\\" MIPStarRE/LDT/SelfImprovement/Defs.lean || true\\n```\\n\\n### Check for proof holes in the whole project\\n\\n```bash\\nrg -n \\\"sorry|axiom\\\" MIPStarRE\\n```\\n\\n### Declaration checker\\n\\n```bash\\nlake exe checkdecls blueprint/lean_decls\\n```\\n\\nUse this when blueprint declaration lists need to match Lean declarations.\\n\\n### Blueprint build\\n\\n```bash\\nleanblueprint pdf    # PDF output\\nleanblueprint web    # HTML output (use as default for quick checks)\\n```\\n\\nIn CI, blueprint linting effectively runs `leanblueprint web` from `blueprint/`.\\n\\n### What counts as a single test\\n\\nThis repository does not have a conventional unit-test suite. The closest\\nsingle-test commands are:\\n\\n- **Lean work**: `lake env lean path/to/File.lean` and\\n  `rg -n \\\"sorry|axiom\\\" path/to/File.lean || true`\\n- **Blueprint work**: `leanblueprint web`\\n- **Whole-repo verification**: `lake build` and\\n  `lake exe checkdecls blueprint/lean_decls`\\n\\n### Recommended validation sequence\\n\\nFor a Lean file change:\\n\\n1. Type-check the edited file with `lake env lean ...`\\n2. Scan that file for `sorry|axiom`\\n3. If the change affects imports or shared declarations, run `lake build`\\n\\nFor blueprint changes:\\n\\n1. Run `leanblueprint web`\\n2. If declaration links changed, run `lake exe checkdecls blueprint/lean_decls`\\n\\n## Toolchain\\n\\n- **Lean**: v4.31.0 (from `lean-toolchain`)\\n- **Mathlib**: v4.31.0 (from `lakefile.toml`)\\n\\nImportant `lakefile.toml` options:\\n\\n- `relaxedAutoImplicit = false` — declare all variables explicitly\\n- `pp.unicode.fun = true`\\n- `weak.linter.mathlibStandardSet = true`\\n- `maxSynthPendingDepth = 3`\\n\\n## Proof-Filling Order\\n\\nSections must be filled in this dependency order:\\n\\n1. Sections 3–4: test setup and preliminaries\\n2. Section 5: making measurements projective\\n3. Sections 7–8: expansion and global variance\\n4. Section 9: self-improvement\\n5. Sections 10–11: commutativity\\n6. Section 12: pasting\\n7. Section 6: main induction wrapper\\n\\nDo not start from the final theorem and guess intermediate facts.\\n\\n## Faithful Formalization Policy\\n\\nA declaration is a formalization of a paper theorem only when its public Lean\\nstatement matches the cited paper statement, up to faithful formal encoding.\\nChanging a Lean theorem away from the corresponding statement in\\n`references/ldt-paper/` is strongly discouraged and should occur only when a\\nfaithful formal encoding or a documented mathematical necessity requires it.\\nThis applies to every declaration advertised as a formalization of a paper\\nresult, not only to theorems currently undergoing repair.  The check is on the\\nhypotheses as well as the conclusion.  A theorem whose\\nconclusion has the right shape but whose assumptions include an extra\\nload-bearing bridge input or hypotheses bundle is a conditional theorem, not\\nthe paper theorem.\\nThe project goal is to eliminate such conditional bridges, not to normalize\\nthem as permanent infrastructure.\\n\\nIf the only available Lean theorem has extra assumptions, the blueprint must\\nnot mark the source-labelled paper entry as matched by that theorem.  Either\\nleave the source-labelled entry without `\\\\leanok`, or state the restricted or\\nconditional result as a separate Lean-only blueprint entry whose hypotheses are\\ndisplayed explicitly.  A scope-restricted theorem may be marked `\\\\leanok` only\\nagainst a blueprint statement that explicitly states the restriction; it must\\nnot be presented as the unrestricted source theorem.\\n\\nWhen a stricter-hypothesis Lean theorem is the only available version near a\\nsource theorem, the discrepancy must have a paper-gap note or tracking issue.\\nThe note should identify the missing hypothesis, explain why it is not part of\\nthe cited statement, and state the plan for eliminating the restriction,\\nderiving it internally, or moving the result out of the source-labelled route.\\n\\nThis rule applies especially to declarations named after paper labels such as\\n`mainFormal`, `selfImprovement`, `mainInduction`, or other theorem names linked\\nfrom the blueprint by `\\\\lean{...}` and `\\\\leanok`.\\n\\nBefore editing any theorem tagged with a paper label (`thm:*`, `lem:*`,\\n`prop:*`):\\n\\n1. Read the corresponding statement in `references/ldt-paper/`.\\n2. Preserve the public Lean theorem statement, except for hypotheses that are\\n   genuinely part of the faithful encoding of the paper's domain.\\n3. Do not add bridge inputs, residual packages, repair hypotheses, producer\\n   assumptions, generic hypotheses or assumptions bundles, or arbitrary\\n   implication hypotheses to the paper theorem.\\n4. If a missing intermediate fact is needed, first state that fact as a named\\n   lemma or theorem to be proved from the paper hypotheses.\\n5. Do not add a conditional helper merely to keep a file compiling.  A\\n   conditional helper may remain temporarily only when the proof content it\\n   preserves is mathematically useful, the source-faithful theorem remains\\n   visible, and the helper has a paper-gap note, a named construction theorem\\n   or proof-obligation target, and an explicit discharge or deletion plan.  Its\\n   name must show that it is conditional without making the assumption look like\\n   an acceptable source hypothesis, for example\\n   `mainFormal_ofInternalObligations`, `selfImprovementFromObligations`, or a\\n   name ending in `_ofObligations`.\\n6. Do not point a source-labelled blueprint theorem to the conditional helper\\n   with `\\\\leanok`.\\n\\nWhen reviewing an existing bridge, residual, repair, producer, or package\\nhypothesis, first try to recover any genuine proof content from its construction\\nand turn that content into a source-faithful lemma.  If the bridge does not\\nactually follow from the paper hypotheses, do not preserve the paper theorem as\\na strengthened statement.  Restore the paper-aligned theorem statement and leave\\nthe missing proof obligation explicit, even if that means reintroducing a\\ntracked `sorry` during a repair PR.\\n\\nSome side conditions are not deviations: positivity needed to define a\\ndivision, nonemptiness of a finite type, decidability instances, field-model\\ninstances, and similar boundary hypotheses may be faithful encodings of\\nassumptions that the paper leaves implicit.  These should still be reviewed and\\ndocumented if they are mathematically load-bearing.  The forbidden pattern is\\ndifferent: moving an unproved step of the proof into the theorem statement, such\\nas a `BridgeHypotheses`, `Input`, `Residual`, `Package`, `RepairInput`,\\n`Producer`, generic `Hypotheses`, or generic `Assumptions` assumption that the\\npaper theorem does not assume.  These assumptions\\nshould not be introduced merely to keep a file compiling or to avoid a `sorry`;\\nthey require explicit mathematical justification and a planned discharge.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/home/drx/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr533/e371f2277c70aed116624161a1083fecc11b363d/code-last-message.md.dispatch.log:9:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' docs/CONTRIBUTING.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Contributing to MIPStarRE\\n\\nThis document codifies the conventions for pull requests, issues, code review,\\nLean style, and CI automation used in the MIPStarRE project.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Pull Request Conventions\\n\\n### Title format\\n\\nUse **conventional-commit** style:\\n\\n```\\ntype(scope): short description\\n```\\n\\n| Type       | When to use                                      |\\n|------------|--------------------------------------------------|\\n| `feat`     | New definition, lemma, theorem, or module         |\\n| `fix`      | Bug fix (broken proof, wrong identifier, etc.)    |\\n| `refactor` | Restructuring without changing API surface        |\\n| `docs`     | Documentation or blueprint changes only           |\\n| `style`    | Formatting, naming, or docstring cleanup only     |\\n| `ci`       | CI/CD workflow changes                            |\\n| `chore`    | Dependency bumps, linting, toolchain updates      |\\n\\n**Scope** is a shortened module path: `LDT/SelfImprovement`, `Quantum`, `blueprint`,\\n`LDT/Pasting`, etc. Omit the `MIPStarRE/` prefix.\\n\\nExamples:\\n- `feat(LDT/SelfImprovement): add self-improvement induction step`\\n- `fix(blueprint): resolve broken labels in chapter 7`\\n- `refactor(LDT): extract shared preliminaries into Basic module`\\n\\n### Body template\\n\\nEvery PR body must contain three sections. For mathematical changes, the body\\nshould be readab…29201 tokens truncated…th{lem:qld-nonencoding-mass-bound}\\n   121\\tseparately records the estimate on the remaining outcomes.\\n   122\\t\\n   123\\tThe declarations \\\\path{decodeFq_add}, \\\\path{decodeFq_smul}, and\\n   124\\t\\\\path{decodeFq_lowDegreeEncoding} in\\n   125\\t\\\\path{MIPStarRE/QPBT/Algebra/Decoding.lean} match the three algebraic assertions.\\n   126\\tIn the same file, \\\\path{IsEncoding} is the equality\\n   127\\t$g_{\\\\operatorname{Dec}(g)}=g$ of polynomial representatives, and\\n   128\\t\\\\path{decodeFq_dotProduct_indicatorVec} requires an explicit hypothesis\\n   129\\tof that predicate.  It therefore matches the corrected evaluation lemma and\\n   130\\tdoes not assert the false unrestricted identity.  Finally,\\n   131\\t\\\\path{nonencodingMarginalMass_le} in\\n   132\\t\\\\path{MIPStarRE/QPBT/Extraction/Consistency.lean} concludes the complementary\\n   133\\tmass bound from the projective strategy and global polynomial-pair witness; it\\n   134\\tdoes not add the bound as a witness field or theorem hypothesis.\\n   135\\t\\n   136\\tThe decoder calculations are proved.  \\\\path{lowDegreeEncoding_mem_poly},\\n   137\\t\\\\path{decodeFq_add}, \\\\path{decodeFq_smul}, \\\\path{decodeFq_lowDegreeEncoding},\\n   138\\tand \\\\path{decodeFq_dotProduct_indicatorVec} carry complete proofs: the degree\\n   139\\tside-condition on $g_h$, the two linearity laws, the inversion\\n   140\\t$\\\\operatorname{Dec}_{\\\\mathbb F_q}(g_h)=h$, and the evaluation identity under the\\n   141\\texplicit \\\\path{IsEncoding} hypothesis.  The complementary estimate\\n   142\\t\\\\path{nonencodingMarginalMass_le} is the remaining open proof of this note.\\n   143\\tIssue~\\\\#19 owns the Stage~4.2 statements and this discrepancy record;\\n   144\\t\\\\ghissue{47} owns the Stage~4.3 decoder calculations, now discharged, and the\\n   145\\tnon-encoding support estimate.  No unrestricted theorem, residual assumption, or\\n   146\\tadditional witness field was introduced.\\n   147\\t\\n   148\\t\\\\section{Conclusion}\\n   149\\t\\n   150\\tThe decoding identity is false for a general polynomial outcome.  Restricting\\n   151\\tit to the encoding image is a necessary local correction.  Recovering the\\n   152\\tsource's measurement-consistency conclusion then requires the separate\\n   153\\tnon-encoding-mass estimate, which remains an open proof obligation.\\n   154\\t\\n   155\\t\\\\bibliographystyle{alpha}\\n   156\\t\\\\bibliography{references}\\n   157\\t\\n   158\\t\\\\end{document}\\n     1\\t\\\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\t\\\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\\n     4\\t\\\\usepackage{xurl}\\n     5\\t\\\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\\n     6\\t\\\\setlength{\\\\emergencystretch}{2em}\\n     7\\t\\n     8\\t\\\\input{command}\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\t\\\\title{Documenting Possible Gaps, Counterexamples, and Formalization Deviations}\\n    11\\t\\\\author{}\\n    12\\t\\\\date{2026-04-29}\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\t\\\\begin{document}\\n    15\\t\\\\maketitle\\n    16\\t\\n    17\\t\\\\section{Purpose}\\n    18\\t\\n    19\\tWhen a formalization relies on a cited mathematical source, the cited source\\n    20\\tshould remain intact.  It is a source, not an errata file.  If a possible gap,\\n    21\\tmissing hypothesis, incorrect numerical constant, or other mathematical\\n    22\\tdiscrepancy is found, the matter should be recorded in a separate LaTeX note.\\n    23\\tIn this repository such notes live under \\\\path{docs/paper-gaps/}.  The same\\n    24\\tprinciple applies when the formal development has already diverged from the\\n    25\\tcited source, for instance by changing a statement so that it compiles, or by\\n    26\\tusing a different mathematical route because an available formal-library theorem\\n    27\\thas a slightly different form.  Such a note is a mathematical note first, and a\\n    28\\trecord of the formalization second: it should be readable by a mathematician or\\n    29\\tmathematical physicist who has not followed the issue discussion.\\n    30\\t\\n    31\\tThe note should isolate the mathematical mechanism.  It should say what the\\n    32\\tcited source asserts, what the cited results actually imply, where a term or\\n    33\\thypothesis is lost, and what statement the formal development proves instead.\\n    34\\tIf the formal proof takes a different route, the note should explain the\\n    35\\treplacement argument and the hypotheses on which it rests.  This is especially\\n    36\\timportant when the proof uses a theorem, construction, or convention from\\n    37\\tMathlib which is not part of the cited argument.  In that case the note should\\n    38\\tstate the Mathlib result in ordinary mathematical language, explain how it is\\n    39\\tspecialized to the notation of the paper, and compare its hypotheses and\\n    40\\tconclusion with the step it replaces.  It should not merely say that \\\\Lean{} did\\n    41\\tnot accept a proof, that the modified proof compiles, or that a named library\\n    42\\tlemma was applied.\\n    43\\t\\n    44\\tThe documents in this directory should themselves follow this standard.  A\\n    45\\tpolicy or template which reads like a checklist will tend to produce checklist\\n    46\\tnotes; the examples and templates should therefore be written as mathematical\\n    47\\tprose.\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\t\\\\section{Concision and Readability}\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\tA paper-gap note should be short enough that a reader can understand the issue\\n    52\\tin one sitting.  The first page should answer four questions: what the source\\n    53\\tuses, how hard the remaining formal work is, what the formalization or audit\\n    54\\tchecked, and what the current verdict is.  Begin with a short\\n    55\\t\\\\emph{At a glance} section when the note concerns an open or recently resolved\\n    56\\tformalization task.  That section should estimate the difficulty, the rough\\n    57\\timplementation weight, the split between Mathlib reuse and project-local work,\\n    58\\tand the key Mathlib APIs or modules.  Use honest ranges, not false precision.\\n    59\\t\\n    60\\tThe \\\\emph{Key theorem forms} section should be Section~2, immediately after\\n    61\\t\\\\emph{At a glance}.  Put the theorem forms before the source details so the\\n    62\\treader knows what the formalization is aiming to prove.  After that, keep the\\n    63\\tsource assertion, the mechanism being checked, the blueprint/formal comparison,\\n    64\\tand the conclusion.  Use more sections only when a counterexample or long\\n    65\\tderivation genuinely needs room.\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\tKeep background to the minimum needed for the local point.  Prefer one clear\\n    68\\tspecial case over a general restatement of the surrounding theorem.  Use displayed\\n    69\\tequations for the source assertion, the key calculation, and the theorem forms;\\n    70\\tavoid displays for traceability data, declaration names, or long lists.  The\\n    71\\tblueprint/formal comparison should be compact and should not repeat the theorem\\n    72\\tforms.  The section ``Form of the Note'' below specifies where traceability data\\n    73\\tbelongs; the body should read as mathematical prose, not as a log.\\n    74\\t\\n    75\\tIf the verdict is that there is no mathematical discrepancy, say so plainly and\\n    76\\tstop.  Keep the conclusion highly compact, normally under twenty source lines.\\n    77\\tIf the only remaining work is an implementation task, name it briefly and link\\n    78\\tthe issue or pull request in a footnote.\\n    79\\t\\n    80\\t\\\\section{When the Investigation Starts}\\n    81\\t\\n    82\\tThe investigation starts when the obstruction appears to be mathematical rather\\n    83\\tthan merely formal.  No final judgement is needed at this stage.  It is enough\\n    84\\tthat, after reading the relevant source and attempting the corresponding formal\\n    85\\tstatement, the proof seems to require a different scalar, an additional\\n    86\\thypothesis, a different tensor placement, a distinction between two objects which\\n    87\\tthe prose identifies, or an auxiliary assertion not supplied by the cited\\n    88\\targument.\\n    89\\t\\n    90\\tThe same investigation should also start when such a deviation is discovered\\n    91\\tafter the fact.  If the existing formal development or informal blueprint has\\n    92\\talready changed the statement, strengthened a hypothesis, weakened a conclusion,\\n    93\\tinserted an unadvertised lemma, or replaced the cited proof by a different\\n    94\\tformal-library argument, the mathematical change should be documented even if all\\n    95\\trelevant files already compile.\\n    96\\t\\n    97\\tAt this early stage the purpose is to preserve the question while it is still\\n    98\\tlocal.  The note may later conclude that the matter is only notational, that an\\n    99\\tintermediate estimate in the cited source needs a harmless correction, that a\\n   100\\tmissing hypothesis is genuinely needed, or that no discrepancy remains.  Thus\\n   101\\tthe trigger is not the severity of the eventual verdict, but the appearance of a\\n   102\\treal mismatch between the cited reasoning and the statement that can be\\n   103\\tformalized.\\n   104\\t\\n   105\\tA failed tactic search, a missing formal lemma, or an inconvenient coercion is\\n   106\\tnot enough.  The obstruction must survive translation back into ordinary\\n   107\\tmathematics.\\n   108\\t\\n   109\\tOnce a cited statement is established to be mathematically false, the repair\\n   110\\tand escalation procedure is governed by Section~6 of\\n   111\\t\\\\path{local/protocols/issues-prs.md}.  Before asking the human owner to choose a\\n   112\\treplacement, a dedicated mathematical-gap session must produce a correction\\n   113\\twhich is correct, sufficient for every paper and blueprint use, and minimal,\\n   114\\tthen iterate it against the Lean statement and its downstream consumers.  A\\n   115\\tClaude Fable~5.1 session is currently launched by the owner session through its\\n   116\\tAgent tool; a Codex main session requests that lane on progress log~\\\\#27 rather\\n   117\\tthan dispatching a Codex worker.  Only after the astra poller reports\\n   118\\tavailability on owner inbox~\\\\#26 does the Codex route become\\n   119\\t\\\\texttt{dispatch.sh --role mathfix} with astra.  A change to a mathematical\\n   120\\tdefinition or game is escalated immediately; other repairs reach the owner\\n   121\\tinbox only after the shared convergence budget expires.\\n   122\\t\\n   123\\tSometimes an investigation uncovers an improvement that is not a discrepancy:\\n   124\\tthe formalization works correctly, but it could be simplified by using an\\n   125\\texisting library, replacing a custom type, or adopting a cleaner proof route.\\n   126\\tThese are not, by themselves, paper-gap notes.  They are action items.  When\\n   127\\tsuch an improvement is identified, open a GitHub issue or pull request that\\n   128\\tdescribes the proposed change.  If the investigation also produces a paper-gap\\n   129\\tnote, link the issue or pull request from that note: the note records the\\n   130\\tmathematical justification, and the issue or pull request drives the\\n   131\\timplementation.\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\t\\\\section{When to Write a Note}\\n   134\\t\\n   135\\tA note should be written once the source argument and the formal proof no longer\\n   136\\tmatch as mathematical arguments.  This includes, for instance, a numerical\\n   137\\testimate whose constant does not follow from the cited lemma, a displayed\\n   138\\tconclusion which uses an unstated hypothesis, or a step in which two objects are\\n   139\\tidentified although they must remain distinct.  In analytic, algebraic,\\n   140\\tprobabilistic, or quantum arguments, such distinctions may concern the domain of\\n   141\\ta map, the placement of a tensor factor, the type of an outcome, the distinction\\n   142\\tbetween a structure and a substructure, or the scalar carried by an approximation\\n   143\\trelation.\\n   144\\t\\n   145\\tA note is required when an audit finds that a Lean declaration associated with a\\n   146\\tsource-labelled statement has acquired an extra non-source hypothesis, such as\\n   147\\tbridge, residual, repair, producer, or obligation-structure data.  Such a\\n   148\\tdeclaration is temporary proof debt, not a replacement for the source theorem,\\n   149\\tand should not be introduced merely to avoid a \\\\verb|sorry|.  The note must say\\n   150\\tplainly that the Lean result is conditional, identify the source-faithful theorem\\n   151\\tform that remains to be proved, and give the intended obligation discharger or\\n   152\\tremoval plan.  The blueprint should not mark the source-labelled statement with\\n   153\\t\\\\verb|\\\\leanok| by pointing to this conditional helper.\\n   154\\t\\n   155\\tChanging a Lean theorem away from the statement in\\n   156\\t\\\\path{references/ldt-paper/} is strongly discouraged.  A paper-gap note should\\n   157\\tnot make such a change look routine: it should explain the mathematical\\n   158\\tnecessity, or else classify the change as proof debt to be removed.\\n   159\\t\\n   160\\tWhen an existing conditional helper is audited, the first task is to extract any\\n   161\\targument it already contains into source-faithful lemmas.  If the remaining\\n   162\\tbridge hypothesis cannot be derived from the paper hypotheses, the formal\\n   163\\tdevelopment should restore the source theorem statement and record the missing\\n   164\\tproof obligation explicitly, rather than retaining the strengthened statement as\\n   165\\tthough it were the paper theorem.\\n   166\\t\\n   167\\tThis requirement is not meant to classify every explicit Lean side condition as\\n   168\\ta discrepancy.  Positivity, nonemptiness, decidability, field-model instances,\\n   169\\tand denominator nonvanishing hypotheses may be faithful encodings of the domain\\n   170\\tin which the paper's theorem is stated.  The note should distinguish such\\n   171\\tboundary hypotheses from hypotheses that supply an unproved step of the paper\\n   172\\targument.\\n   173\\t\\n   174\\tA note is also appropriate when the proof becomes formal only after changing an\\n   175\\tintermediate scalar, weakening a conclusion, strengthening a hypothesis, or\\n   176\\tinserting an additional lemma which is not simply an expansion of the cited\\n   177\\targument.  These are mathematical changes, even when they are harmless for the\\n   178\\tfinal theorem.  It is likewise appropriate when an available formal-library\\n   179\\ttheorem proves the needed result under a somewhat different formulation from the\\n   180\\tcited source.  In that case the note should be deliberately pedagogical.  It\\n   181\\tshould explain, in ordinary mathematical language, what the replacement result\\n   182\\tsays, how it is specialized, which hypotheses are new or differently packaged,\\n   183\\tand why the resulting statement is a legitimate substitute for the step in the\\n   184\\tcited argument.  If the issue remains open after reasonable attempts, the note\\n   185\\tshould say what has been checked and what remains unknown.\\n   186\\t\\n   187\\t\\\\section{Counterexamples}\\n   188\\t\\n   189\\tIf the cited assertion appears to be false, the note should not stop at saying\\n   190\\tthat the proof cannot be formalized.  It should give the counterexample, or the\\n   191\\tsmallest relevant part of it, in ordinary mathematical notation.  The example\\n   192\\tshould be accompanied by enough verification that the reader can see both sides:\\n   193\\tthe hypotheses of the cited assertion hold, and its conclusion fails.\\n   194\\t\\n   195\\tWhen feasible, the counterexample should also be formalized in \\\\Lean{}.  This is\\n   196\\tespecially valuable when the example is finite, algebraic, computational, or\\n   197\\totherwise small enough that the failure can be checked without reproducing the\\n   198\\twhole surrounding theory.  The note should then state the mathematical content\\n   199\\tof the checked counterexample and identify the corresponding Lean declaration in\\n   200\\ta footnote.  The Lean file is not a substitute for the prose: the prose should\\n   201\\tstill explain why the example refutes the cited assertion.\\n   202\\t\\n   203\\t\\\\section{Revision of a Note}\\n   204\\t\\n   205\\tA paper-gap note may be provisional.  It may begin as soon as the mathematical\\n   206\\tobstruction has been isolated well enough to state the question, and it may be\\n   207\\trevised as the calculation, the blueprint, or the corresponding \\\\Lean{}\\n   208\\tdeclarations become clearer.  The first version need not know the final\\n   209\\tclassification, but it should say plainly which mathematical point is known and\\n   210\\twhich point remains unsettled.\\n   211\\t\\n   212\\tLater revisions should not make the note into a chronological log.  They should\\n   213\\trewrite the document so that it remains a coherent account of the present\\n   214\\tmathematical understanding.  If the verdict changes, the conclusion should state\\n   215\\tthe current verdict; the linked issue or pull request can preserve the earlier\\n   216\\tdiscussion.\\n   217\\t\\n   218\\t\\\\section{Form of the Note}\\n   219\\t\\n   220\\tEach note should stand on its own.  It should give the date, cite the relevant\\n   221\\tsource article, and provide enough traceability for the reader to locate the\\n   222\\toriginal passage and the project discussion.  GitHub issues and pull requests may\\n   223\\tbe linked with \\\\verb|\\\\href|, and source files, line ranges, and labels should be\\n   224\\trecorded when they help the reader check the claim.  These references should\\n   225\\tserve the exposition rather than govern it: the main text should be organized\\n   226\\taround the mathematical assertion and its proof.\\n   227\\t\\n   228\\tThe note should introduce its notation before using it.  Symbols for\\n   229\\tmeasurements, tensor factors, approximation relations, and error parameters\\n   230\\tshould be named in prose.  If an approximation relation is state-dependent, the\\n   231\\tstate should be named, or its defining expression should be given when this can\\n   232\\tbe done briefly.  Only after the notation is in place should the note state the\\n   233\\tassertion in the cited source, using a bibliographic citation such as\\n   234\\t\\\\verb|\\\\cite[Section~...]{...}| when the section or chapter is known.\\n   235\\t\\n   236\\tOnly the relevant hypotheses should be reproduced.  If the question is a scalar\\n   237\\tsubstitution, a missing term, or a missing hypothesis, the note should not quote\\n   238\\ta long unrelated part of the proof.  It should give the cited lemma or\\n   239\\tproposition in the special case actually used, then display the calculation or\\n   240\\tlogical implication in which the discrepancy appears.\\n   241\\t\\n   242\\tDisplayed equations should be used with restraint.  They are appropriate for the\\n   243\\tsource estimate being checked, the cited estimate in the form actually used, and\\n   244\\tthe calculation where a term or hypothesis appears or disappears.  When a\\n   245\\tdisplayed equation is identified by a source label, the label should appear on\\n   246\\tthe right of the display, while the surrounding prose refers to the mathematical\\n   247\\tassertion itself.  The standard \\\\texttt{amsmath} command \\\\verb|\\\\tag| is the\\n   248\\tright mechanism for this; a separate local convention should be introduced only\\n   249\\tif the standard mechanism is genuinely insufficient.  For a multi-line aligned\\n   250\\tdisplay, tag the individual lines that carry the source information rather than\\n   251\\tforcing a long combined label below the equations.  Short\\n   252\\tscalar definitions and elementary inequalities should remain inline when the\\n   253\\tsentence reads better that way.  As in ordinary mathematical writing, a displayed\\n   254\\tformula is part of the surrounding sentence and should be punctuated accordingly.\\n   255\\t\\n   256\\tBibliographic entries used only by these notes should live in\\n   257\\t\\\\path{docs/paper-gaps/references.bib}.  A new note should ordinarily begin from\\n   258\\t\\\\path{docs/paper-gaps/template.tex}, which records the common LaTeX preamble,\\n   259\\tthe local bibliography, and a model for right-hand source labels in displayed\\n   260\\tequations.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a09e58-29ec-7233-b950-156ba1fc0951","create_time":1789363200.624282}},{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_0dc45167a280c00e016aa784056aa487d08993effe8aa78fb7","summary":[],"encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqp4QOciErFfU6OmD0A5pjgOitQPt9JnwWxt8sVJW9MJhR22DFRwIjM0T4WrEXqPgqt0IryQDSmUgsCQDXCbOqnf_JGUlvHHiLQE4sgDJQKLZDTI8efdNZSj-FDfmti-ELtg2Cggc-gKOOHhQmXTvT6_gHIgxHHJg0JgyeRuw7aB72LNoHEKcuc4mbvOd8KncFHAZEJnsDBR_vYIJlmWQk_rtlAzJlDZtSZ13qUB4fSn35k9Ck_jIkunkxA0RLNYZHcgo1GjgX71uWhQi9PXzcHB8nfV9dXs7Xsh7lwJ0DtsnYm6JM3K4GNkEqgDI5qdpE4vNSgWABrKUA2ETPtip9p4TUjVoOn6s2_YNlXnsCoZ_cY96b4bdotmZxNJGWEpHFDmPoK6hx4md61Cjw_dNzwb7wSZ6lcYxrgmvM7lv3jKaRZAA41EL3Gzi08JZGNo9zDGjaiTZswHFcTeLDebVGy4BVaTrLoStzpMgalEOSTKyzJo-TEatndtk84sxUJ1OWr7No3zOC8mt2nrG4yfT8J0QCwuLh5f8TDTTAfk6L1q26sL5gQ9m2WQcoiVu8ekBHEVVFIctGA4Rsccu9y--45q4XyLCmC5CQavhKIHHbpq_1qpadKZIM2lQwxksChD-BdRVCoHyYx4HaP3HKx3xC_-bLG7NlxINiau9ZYvde7lohzwz5RozMh2qw6K6zID1S8XVbIQhc2TbKq4w72VQqXQqfgrf3guTUAJ4xHPzzPkkKhcbYN9tz7ppHuJdAleac2WcURA8spgiuYTJiE2yS5su25azU2w6Iysi2WD8XfWMbDTTSBd9ASt8cs