 YAML front matter. New audit\n    19\tfiles and substantive updates to existing audits should also follow the section\n    20\tformat below.\n    21\t\n    22\t## Metadata\n    23\t\n    24\tEvery audit should begin with YAML front matter in this shape:\n    25\t\n    26\t```yaml\n    27\t---\n    28\ttitle: \"Short human-readable title\"\n    29\tdate: YYYY-MM-DD\n    30\tpurpose: >\n    31\t  One or two sentences explaining what the audit records and how it should be\n    32\t  used by later readers.\n    33\t---\n    34\t```\n    35\t\n    36\tUse `issue` and `pr` when the audit is tied to a specific GitHub issue or pull\n    37\trequest:\n    38\t\n    39\t```yaml\n    40\tissue: \"#N\"\n    41\tpr: \"#M\"\n    42\t```\n    43\t\n    44\tThe values should be quoted strings such as `\"#913\"` so YAML parses them as\n    45\ttext rather than comments.\n    46\t\n    47\tExisting or useful classification fields may be kept. In this repository the\n    48\tcommon ones are:\n    49\t\n    50\t```yaml\n    51\tstatus: active\n    52\ttrack: paper2009ldt\n    53\tkind: naming-audit\n    54\torigin: internal\n    55\t```\n    56\t\n    57\tThese fields are optional metadata, not a replacement for the prose purpose.\n    58\tWhen adding a new audit, prefer the minimal header above unless the extra fields\n    59\thelp later readers sort or review the audit.\n    60\t\n    61\t## File Names\n    62\t\n    63\tDated audit artifacts should use a leading `YYYY-MM-DD_` filename. For example,\n    64\tuse `2026-04-29_mathematical-naming-audit.md`, not\n    65\t`mathematical-naming-audit_2026-04-29.md` and not\n    66\t`20260429_mathematical-naming-audit.md`.\n    67\t\n    68\t## Required Sections\n    69\t\n    70\tUse these sections unless the audit is deliberately narrower:\n    71\t\n    72\t- `Scope`: the files, declarations, prose, or mathematical objects audited.\n    73\t- `Source of Truth`: the paper, blueprint chapter, or repository convention used\n    74\t  to decide the mathematical terminology.\n    75\t- `Findings`: the observed mismatches, grouped by mathematical topic or API\n    76\t  surface.\n    77\t- `Required Action`: the precise cleanup or migration needed before the audit\n    78\t  can be marked resolved.\n    79\t- `Validation`: the Lean, blueprint, or documentation checks relevant to the\n    80\t  audit.\n    81\t- `Review Use`: how reviewers should apply the audit in later PRs.\n    82\t\n    83\t## Term Norm\n    84\t\n    85\tAudit prose should use terms from the mathematical source, the blueprint, or an\n    86\testablished repository convention. It should not introduce project management\n    87\tphrases as mathematical names. In particular, avoid presenting words such as\n    88\t`pipeline`, `wrapper`, `package`, `raw`, `oneShot`, or `liveBlock` as preferred\n    89\tmathematical terminology unless the cited source uses them with that meaning.\n    90\t\n    91\tWhen an audit names a problematic existing identifier, describe it as a legacy\n    92\tidentifier or a finding. Do not turn an implementation-history word into the\n    93\ttitle of a new concept. Prefer terms such as:\n    94\t\n    95\t- `finding`\n    96\t- `required action`\n    97\t- `legacy identifier`\n    98\t- `source of truth`\n    99\t- `validation`\n   100\t- `review use`\n   101\t- `API migration`\n   102\t\n   103\tFor Lean naming audits, a name is suspect when it encodes historical\n   104\tformalization status rather than mathematical content. If a public name cannot\n   105\tbe changed immediately, record the migration here or in a linked issue, and do\n   106\tnot add an empty pass-through abbreviation just to create a second public name.\n   107\t\n   108\t## Review Rule\n   109\t\n   110\tWhen a review fix touches a surface covered by an active audit, the audit is\n   111\trequired reading. A PR is not ready to merge if it ignores an in-scope required\n   112\taction, leaves the same terminology problem on the touched surface, or replaces\n   113\tit only with an empty pass-through abbreviation. If the PR deliberately defers\n   114\tthe migration, it should update the audit or linked issue with the remaining\n   115\twork and the mathematical reason for the deferral.\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a09e58-29ec-7233-b950-156ba1fc0951","create_time":1789363264.7913089}},{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a09e5c-9d1f-7fb0-921b-e1e4f63ef710","call_id":"call_sDL990ZOdRdrRoYeC2Qp6ZXk","output":"Chunk ID: f4c06a\nWall time: 0.0171 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 4125\nOutput:\n     1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n     2\t\n     3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xurl}\n     5\t\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\n     6\t\\setlength{\\emergencystretch}{2em}\n     7\t\n     8\t\\input{command}\n     9\t\n    10\t\\title{Proof-Gap Protocol for Source-Faithful Formalization}\n    11\t\\author{}\n    12\t\\date{2026-05-11}\n    13\t\n    14\t\\begin{document}\n    15\t\\maketitle\n    16\t\n    17\t\\section{Purpose}\n    18\t\n    19\tThis note fixes terminology for a recurring failure mode in the formalization\n    20\tof the low individual degree test paper, \\path{arXiv:2009.12982}.  It\n    21\tcomplements the note-writing policy in \\path{docs/paper-gaps/policy.tex} and\n    22\tthe source-statement audit tracked historically in \\ghissue{1458}.  The current\n    23\tsource-boundary proof holes are tracked in \\ghissue{1507} and \\ghissue{422}.\n    24\tA proof may be blocked at\n    25\tan intermediate mathematical assertion.  If that assertion is added as a new\n    26\thypothesis to a theorem labelled as a paper theorem, the resulting \\Lean{}\n    27\ttheorem\n    28\tmay be correct as a conditional statement, but it is no longer the theorem in\n    29\tthe cited source.  The apparent proof has not closed the paper theorem; it has\n    30\tmoved a proof obligation into the theorem statement.\n    31\t\n    32\tThe protocol below distinguishes such proof debt from legitimate formal\n    33\tboundary conditions and from genuine mathematical corrections.  It is meant for\n    34\tcontributors and reviewers who must decide whether a bridge, residual, repair,\n    35\tinput, or obligation structure is a proved local construction, a tracked proof\n    36\tgap, or statement drift which must be repaired before a theorem may be\n    37\tadvertised as a source theorem.  The default rule is restrictive: such data\n    38\tmust not be added to a paper-facing theorem unless it is part of the cited\n    39\tsource statement or a documented faithful encoding of its ambient hypotheses.\n    40\t\n    41\t\\section{Basic objects}\n    42\t\n    43\t\\paragraph{Source theorem.}\n    44\tA source theorem is a theorem, lemma, proposition, or corollary in the paper\n    45\tsource under \\path{references/ldt-paper/}.  Its hypotheses and conclusion are\n    46\tthe mathematical ground truth, subject only to faithful formal encoding and to\n    47\tdocumented paper-gap corrections.\n    48\t\n    49\t\\paragraph{Paper-facing declaration.}\n    50\tA \\Lean{} declaration is paper-facing when it is named or cited as the\n    51\tformalization of a source theorem, for instance through a blueprint\n    52\t\\verb|\\lean{}| link on a theorem environment carrying a paper label.  Such a\n    53\tdeclaration must match the source theorem in its public statement.  In\n    54\tparticular, it may not add an unproved bridge, residual, repair, input,\n    55\tobligation structure, or other proof-obligation hypothesis which the paper does\n    56\tnot assume.\n    57\t\n    58\t\\paragraph{Proof obligation.}\n    59\tA proof obligation is an intermediate assertion needed by the proof of a source\n    60\ttheorem.  It may be a construction, an estimate, a transport lemma, or a\n    61\tcomparison between two formal representations.  If the paper proves or invokes\n    62\tthis assertion, then the formalization should state it as a separate lemma or\n    63\ttheorem and prove it from the upstream hypotheses.\n    64\t\n    65\t\\paragraph{Obligation discharger.}\n    66\tAn obligation discharger is a theorem or definition which constructs a proof\n    67\tobligation from the paper hypotheses already available at that point in the\n    68\targument.  This is the preferred representation of missing work.  If the proof\n    69\tis not yet complete, the discharger may temporarily contain a tracked\n    70\t\\verb|sorry|, but the paper-facing theorem should call it rather than assume\n    71\tits output as an additional theorem hypothesis.\n    72\t\n    73\t\\paragraph{Conditional helper.}\n    74\tA conditional helper is a separately named theorem whose statement explicitly\n    75\tassumes a proof obligation.  Names such as\n    76\t\\leanid{mainFormal\\_ofInternalObligations},\n    77\t\\leanid{orthonormalization\\_ofInput}, or names whose suffix advertises the\n    78\tassumed obligation are permitted only when the declaration is not presented as\n    79\tthe source theorem.  A conditional helper is a quarantine for downstream proof\n    80\tcontent; it does not close the source theorem.\n    81\t\n    82\t\\section{Bridge, residual, repair, input, and obligation structures}\n    83\t\n    84\tThe words \\emph{bridge}, \\emph{residual}, \\emph{repair}, \\emph{input}, and\n    85\t\\emph{obligation structure} name possible formal shapes.  They become dangerous\n    86\twhen their mathematical status is unclear or when their names make unproved\n    87\tdata look like acceptable hypotheses of a source theorem.\n    88\t\n    89\t\\paragraph{Bridge.}\n    90\tA bridge should be a transport theorem between two already defined\n    91\tmathematical forms: for example, a tensor-placement identity, a conversion\n    92\tbetween two equivalent consistency relations, or a statement translating a\n    93\tsource object into a formal representation.  A bridge is harmless only after it\n    94\tis proved from previously available assumptions.  It becomes proof debt when it\n    95\tis assumed because the transport has not yet been proved.  It becomes theorem\n    96\tdrift when the assumed bridge is added to a paper-facing theorem.\n    97\t\n    98\t\\paragraph{Residual.}\n    99\tA residual is a named remainder after part of an argument has been performed.\n   100\tIt is often a useful way to record the exact missing target after a completed\n   101\tconstruction.  A residual may appear as the conclusion of a helper theorem or\n   102\tas the input of a quarantined conditional helper.  It is not a permissible new\n   103\tassumption on a paper theorem unless the paper itself assumes that residual.\n   104\t\n   105\t\\paragraph{Input.}\n   106\tAn input is a named bundle of data or hypotheses consumed by a construction or\n   107\tconditional helper.  It is harmless only when each load-bearing field is either\n   108\tpart of the source hypotheses or is produced by an earlier theorem.  It becomes\n   109\tproof debt when the bundle is merely assumed because the construction of its\n   110\tfields is missing.  It becomes theorem drift when such a bundle is added to the\n   111\tpublic statement of a source theorem.\n   112\t\n   113\t\\paragraph{Repair.}\n   114\tA repair is a mathematical construction which replaces an approximate or\n   115\tpartial object by a better object, usually with a quantitative estimate.  In\n   116\tthe low individual degree test formalization, projectivization and\n   117\torthonormalization repairs are genuine mathematical operations.  A repair\n   118\tshould therefore appear as a construction or as a theorem discharging the\n   119\tcorresponding obligation.  If a theorem assumes the output of the repair instead\n   120\tof constructing it, that theorem is conditional on the repair.\n   121\t\n   122\t\\paragraph{Obligation structure.}\n   123\tAn obligation structure is a record whose fields are the data and estimates\n   124\tneeded by a later construction.  This is acceptable only as an internal target\n   125\tor as a hypothesis of a conditional helper.  Such a structure becomes statement\n   126\tdrift if it is added to a paper-facing theorem instead of being constructed\n   127\tinside the proof.  The review question is whether every load-bearing field is\n   128\tproved from the source hypotheses or is explicitly tracked as a missing\n   129\tobligation.\n   130\t\n   131\t\\section{The main failure mode}\n   132\t\n   133\tSuppose the paper proves\n   134\t\\[\n   135\t  H \\Longrightarrow C.\n   136\t\\]\n   137\tDuring formalization, the proof may need an intermediate assertion \\(B\\).  The\n   138\tpaper theorem should be represented as\n   139\t\\[\n   140\t  H \\Longrightarrow B \\Longrightarrow C\n   141\t  \\quad\\text{inside the proof,}\n   142\t\\]\n   143\twhere \\(B\\) is obtained by a lemma or theorem from \\(H\\).  The following\n   144\tpublic statement is a different theorem:\n   145\t\\[\n   146\t  H \\land B \\Longrightarrow C.\n   147\t\\]\n   148\tIt is not unsound in \\Lean{}, but it is not the paper theorem.  It says that the\n   149\tconclusion follows if the missing bridge data is supplied.  Such a theorem is a\n   150\tconditional helper, even if its conclusion looks exactly like the paper's\n   151\tconclusion.\n   152\t\n   153\tThis is the situation colloquially described as ``adding a bridge\n   154\thypothesis''.  More precisely, an unproved intermediate step has been promoted\n   155\tfrom the proof body to the theorem's assumptions.  The correction is not to hide\n   156\tthe hypothesis under a different structure name.  The correction is to keep a\n   157\tpaper-facing theorem with the source statement and move the missing step to a\n   158\tnamed internal proof obligation.\n   159\t\n   160\t\\section{Faithful boundary hypotheses}\n   161\t\n   162\tNot every extra formal hypothesis is a theorem drift.  Some hypotheses are\n   163\tfaithful boundary conditions introduced by formal encoding.  Examples include\n   164\tnonnegativity needed for a real power, positivity needed to divide by a\n   165\tquantity, finiteness and nonemptiness of an index type, a field-model instance,\n   166\tor decidability instances required by \\Lean{}.  Such hypotheses are acceptable\n   167\twhen they express part of the mathematical ambient setting or make explicit a\n   168\tcondition used implicitly in the paper.\n   169\t\n   170\tThe distinction is load-bearing.  A faithful boundary hypothesis describes the\n   171\tdomain in which the paper's objects live.  A proof-debt hypothesis supplies a\n   172\tmathematical step that the proof should derive.  If removing the hypothesis\n   173\tleaves an unproved construction or estimate from the paper proof, then the\n   174\thypothesis is proof debt, not a boundary condition.\n   175\t\n   176\t\\section{Definition drift and subtree repair}\n   177\t\n   178\tDefinition drift is especially dangerous because it propagates.  If a\n   179\tdefinition is changed so that it no longer denotes the object in the paper,\n   180\tthen every downstream theorem using that definition may continue to compile\n   181\twhile proving statements about the wrong object.  Downstream theorem statements\n   182\tcan then be repaired piecemeal, but the development remains unstable because\n   183\tthe wrong definition is still upstream.\n   184\t\n   185\tWhen a definition mismatch is found, the repair should proceed by a subtree\n   186\tprotocol.\n   187\t\n   188\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   189\t  \\item Identify the source object and the formal object precisely.\n   190\t  \\item Freeze a source-faithful definition or state an adapter theorem\n   191\t  relating the existing definition to the source object.\n   192\t  \\item Recheck the downstream theorem chain against that definition or adapter.\n   193\t  \\item Re-formalize the affected subtree from the corrected interface, rather\n   194\t  than adding local hypotheses to each downstream theorem.\n   195\t  \\item If a downstream proof cannot be completed, record the missing obligation\n   196\t  at the lowest point where the paper proof needs it.\n   197\t\\end{enumerate}\n   198\t\n   199\tThis is slower than editing a final theorem signature, but it prevents a local\n   200\tdefinition error from becoming a global theorem-statement error.\n   201\t\n   202\t\\section{Allowed shapes}\n   203\t\n   204\t\\begin{center}\n   205\t\\begin{tabular}{p{0.24\\linewidth}p{0.31\\linewidth}p{0.32\\linewidth}}\n   206\t\\textbf{Shape} & \\textbf{Allowed use} & \\textbf{Forbidden use} \\\\\n   207\t\\hline\n   208\tPaper-facing theorem & Matches the source statement up to faithful encoding &\n   209\tCarries non-paper bridge, residual, repair, input, obligation-structure, or\n   210\tproof-obligation hypotheses \\\\\n   211\tSource-shaped theorem with a tracked \\verb|sorry| & Default repair when the\n   212\tsource proof is blocked but the statement is known & Replaced by a strengthened\n   213\ttheorem statement merely to keep the file compiling \\\\\n   214\tConditional helper & Temporary quarantine for substantial proof content whose\n   215\tremaining named obligation has not yet been discharged & Introduced merely to\n   216\tavoid a \\verb|sorry|, or linked as the source theorem or marked as\n   217\tproof-complete for that theorem \\\\\n   218\tObligation discharger theorem & Constructs the obligation from paper hypotheses &\n   219\tMerely restates the obligation as an assumption \\\\\n   220\tInput bundle & Collects produced data for a local construction & Is assumed by a\n   221\tsource theorem instead of being produced \\\\\n   222\tObligation structure & Bundles constructed data and estimates & Hides unproved\n   223\tfields that are then assumed by a source theorem \\\\\n   224\tPaper-gap note & Documents a real mathematical or formal-statement divergence &\n   225\tJustifies silent statement drift without a repair plan\n   226\t\\end{tabular}\n   227\t\\end{center}\n   228\t\n   229\t\\section{Protocol for a blocked proof}\n   230\t\n   231\tWhen a source-labelled proof is blocked, use the following order.\n   232\t\n   233\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   234\t  \\item Read the cited source statement and the proof passage in\n   235\t  \\path{references/ldt-paper/}.\n   236\t  \\item Write the paper theorem form, the current \\Lean{} theorem form, and the\n   237\t  first missing intermediate assertion.\n   238\t  \\item Decide whether the missing assertion is a faithful boundary condition,\n   239\t  a representation adapter, an ordinary lemma from the paper proof, or a real\n   240\t  paper-gap discrepancy.\n   241\t  \\item If it is an ordinary lemma or adapter, create a named theorem which\n   242\t  discharges that obligation.  Do not add it to the source theorem as a new\n   243\t  hypothesis.\n   244\t  \\item Keep the source-labelled theorem itself in the source form, even if its\n   245\t  proof must contain a tracked \\verb|sorry|.  This is the default repair when\n   246\t  the missing assertion has not yet been proved.\n   247\t  \\item Create a conditional helper only to quarantine substantial proof content\n   248\t  that already exists and would otherwise be lost.  Do not create such a helper\n   249\t  merely to avoid a \\verb|sorry| or to keep making progress.  If a conditional\n   250\t  helper remains, give it a conditional name, keep it separate from the\n   251\t  paper-facing theorem, and record the obligation discharger that must eliminate\n   252\t  it.\n   253\t  \\item If the source and formal statements genuinely differ, write or update a\n   254\t  note under \\path{docs/paper-gaps/} before marking the statement as aligned.\n   255\t  \\item Update the blueprint so that paper labels point only to paper-facing\n   256\t  declarations.  Conditional helpers belong in remarks or implementation\n   257\t  notes, not in the source theorem block.\n   258\t\\end{enumerate}\n   259\t\n   260\t\\section{Retirement criterion}\n   170\t  sorry\n   171\t\n   172\t/-- Construction of the projective global polynomial-pair measurements from\n   173\t`lem:qld-4-7`, paper lines 1267--1274.  The statement has the source's universal\n   174\tconstants and contains no divisibility hypothesis or residual construction input.\n   175\t\n   176\tThe argument uses low-degree soundness at dimension `2 * m + 2`.  The current\n   177\tdirect carrier realizes the required dimension without assuming\n   178\t`2 * m + 2 ∣ q`.  `exists_direct_ld_soundness` proves that soundness statement\n   179\tfor the directly indexed game by applying `MIPStarRE.LDT.Test.mainFormal` and\n   180\tverifying `400 M d <= N` at each LDT application dimension `M`, with sampling\n   181\tcount `N = 2560000 M^3 d`.  The source import at seed-indexed dimension `M`\n   182\tinstead chooses `K = M^3 d` for a tensor-code theorem requiring\n   183\t`K >= 12 M (d + 1)`.  The direct proof establishes neither that bound nor the\n   184\tclaimed tensor-code game correspondence.  Both source-import obligations\n   185\tremain open and are documented in\n   186\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility.tex`.  Absorption of the established\n   187\tcombined-lines prefactor into the final universal constants is to use\n   188\t`deltaQld_mono` on its stated source parameter domain.\n   189\t-/\n   190\ttheorem exists_globalPairWitness :\n   191\t    ∃ a b : ℝ, 1 < a ∧ 0 < b ∧ b < 1 ∧\n   192\t      ∀ (P : AdmissibleParams) (ε : ℝ), 0 < ε →\n   193\t        ∀ S : ProjectiveSetting P ε,\n   194\t          Nonempty (GlobalPairWitness S (deltaQld a b ε P.m P.d P.q)) := by\n   195\t  sorry\n   196\t\n   197\tend\n   198\t\n   199\tend MIPStarRE.QPBT\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a09e58-29ec-7233-b950-156ba1fc0951","create_time":1789363264.7993703}},{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a09e5c-9d21-73e1-866a-56373678d394","call_id":"call_dbPuLbPyaAUJ31lrLulreb0J","output":"Chunk ID: 4fcf94\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 2071\nOutput:\n   210\t\\end{remark}\n   211\t\n   212\t\\section{Consistency with the points measurements}\n   213\t\n   214\tThe next lemma shows that the pulled-apart measurements are consistent with the original points measurements of the strategy, and self-consistent. Write $\\delta_G$ for the error of the global polynomial-pair witness supplied by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}. Assume $0\\leq\\eps\\leq1$ and $\\delta_G\\geq0$, and put\n   215\t\\[\n   216\t\tB = \\delta_G + \\sqrt{\\eps} + \\frac{md}{q}.\n   217\t\\]\n   218\tEach displayed construction estimate below has its own universal constant $C_E \\geq 1$, quantified independently, and its own scale $\\delta_P^E=C_EB$. The source uses one enlarged error $\\delta_S$ for all of these estimates. The two presentations agree: after the finitely many estimates have been established, take $C_* = \\max_E C_E$ and $\\delta_P=C_*B$. Since $B\\geq0$, every bound remains valid at this common scale. In the proof paragraphs, $\\delta_P$ denotes this common enlargement. It has the same asymptotic form as $\\delta_G$: after shrinking $b$ to at most $1/2$, the term $\\eps^b$ dominates $\\sqrt{\\eps}$ for $0 \\leq \\eps \\leq 1$, while $(md)^a q^{-b}$ dominates $md/q$.\n   219\t\n   220\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency and self-consistency of the pulled-apart measurements]\\label{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}\n   221\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.tildeM_consistent_pointMeas}\n   222\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.tildeM_consistent_pointMeas'}\n   223\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.tildeObs_selfConsistent}\n   224\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.deltaConstructPaulis}\n   225\t\t\\leanok\n   226\t\t\\uses{def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tilde-w-observables,\n   227\t\t\tlem:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:consistency,\n   228\t\t\tdef:approx-question-indexed-operators, lem:qld-4-7,\n   229\t\t\tdef:indicator-vector}\n   230\t\tThe measurements $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the following properties.\n   231\t\t% The paper's statement also quantifies item 1 over j, but j does not occur\n   232\t\t% in item 1; the vacuous quantifier is dropped here.\n   233\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   234\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   235\t\t\t\\[\n   236\t\t\t\t\\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\simeq_{\\delta_P^{\\mathrm{AB}}} M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}\n   237\t\t\t\\]\n   238\t\t\tand\n   239\t\t\t\\[\n   240\t\t\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} \\simeq_{\\delta_P^{\\mathrm{BA}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a\\;,\n   241\t\t\t\\]\n   242\t\t\twith the answer summation over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   243\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and $j \\in \\{0,\\ldots,t-1\\}$, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^{M}$,\n   244\t\t\t\\[\n   245\t\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} \\approx_{\\delta_P^{\\mathrm{obs}}} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})\\;.\n   246\t\t\t\\]\n   247\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   248\t\\end{lemma}\n   249\t\\begin{proof}\n   250\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tau-dot-product-projector, lem:qld-decoder-evaluation, lem:qld-decoder-linearity, def:indicator-vector, lem:qld-win-implications, lem:qld-comm-cons, fact:add-a-proj, fact:agreement, fact:triangle, fact:data-processing, def:bracket, lem:povm-to-obs, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}\n   800\t        0 ≤ epsilon → epsilon ≤ 1 → 0 ≤ deltaG →\n   801\t          ∀ (S : ProjectiveSetting P epsilon)\n   802\t            (w : GlobalPairWitness S deltaG)\n   803\t            (side : PlayerSide) (W : PauliKind),\n   804\t            nonencodingMarginalMass w side W ≤\n   805\t              deltaConstructPaulis C epsilon deltaG P.m P.d P.q := by\n   806\t  sorry\n   807\t\n   808\t/-! ## Consistency of the pulled-apart measurements -/\n   809\t\n   810\t/-- Alice's original point measurement is consistent with Bob's pulled-apart\n   811\tmeasurement on average over uniformly random points. This is the first display\n   812\tof Item 1 in blueprint\n   813\t`lem:qld-construct-the-paulis`, paper\n   814\t`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1463-1492`.\n   815\t\n   816\tThe source reuses `deltaS` after absorbing the non-encoding and game-error\n   817\tterms. The bound keeps the global polynomial-pair witness error `deltaG`\n   818\tseparate in `deltaConstructPaulis`.\n   819\t\n   820\t**Proof obligation:** issue #47 tracks the non-encoding-mass estimate required\n   821\tby the restricted decoder identity; see\n   822\t`docs/paper-gaps/qpbt_decoding-identity.tex`. -/\n   823\ttheorem tildeM_consistent_pointMeas :\n   824\t    ∃ C : ℝ, 1 ≤ C ∧\n   825\t      ∀ (P : AdmissibleParams) (epsilon deltaG : ℝ),\n   826\t        0 ≤ epsilon → epsilon ≤ 1 → 0 ≤ deltaG →\n   827\t          ∀ (S : ProjectiveSetting P epsilon)\n   828\t            (w : GlobalPairWitness S deltaG) (W : PauliKind),\n   829\t            consistencyDefect\n   830\t              (uniformDistribution (Fin P.m → PauliScalar P))\n   831\t              (fun u a =>\n   832\t                S.placePlayer .alice ((S.pointMeas .alice W u).effect a))\n   833\t              (fun u a => S.placeSide .bob\n   834\t                (tildeM w .bob W (indicatorVec u) a))\n   835\t              S.psiHat ≤\n   836\t                deltaConstructPaulis C epsilon deltaG P.m P.d P.q := by\n   837\t  sorry\n   838\t\n   839\t/-- Alice's pulled-apart measurement is consistent with Bob's original point\n   840\tmeasurement on average over uniformly random points. This is the\n   841\tregister-interchanged display of Item 1 in blueprint\n   842\t`lem:qld-construct-the-paulis`, paper\n   843\t`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1463-1492`.\n   844\t\n   845\tThe conclusion uses the same explicit construction scale as the first player\n   846\tordering.\n   847\t\n   848\t**Proof obligation:** issue #47 tracks the player-interchanged\n   849\tnon-encoding-mass argument. -/\n   850\ttheorem tildeM_consistent_pointMeas' :\n   851\t    ∃ C : ℝ, 1 ≤ C ∧\n   852\t      ∀ (P : AdmissibleParams) (epsilon deltaG : ℝ),\n   853\t        0 ≤ epsilon → epsilon ≤ 1 → 0 ≤ deltaG →\n   854\t          ∀ (S : ProjectiveSetting P epsilon)\n   855\t            (w : GlobalPairWitness S deltaG) (W : PauliKind),\n   856\t            consistencyDefect\n   857\t              (uniformDistribution (Fin P.m → PauliScalar P))\n   858\t              (fun u a => S.placeSide .alice\n   859\t                (tildeM w .alice W (indicatorVec u) a))\n   860\t              (fun u a =>\n   861\t                S.placePlayer .bob ((S.pointMeas .bob W u).effect a))\n   862\t              S.psiHat ≤\n   863\t                deltaConstructPaulis C epsilon deltaG P.m P.d P.q := by\n   864\t  sorry\n   865\t\n   866\t/-- The pulled-apart observables on Alice's and Bob's extraction blocks are\n   867\tself-consistent on average over the uniformly random Pauli register. This is\n   868\tItem 2 of blueprint\n   869\t`lem:qld-construct-the-paulis`, paper\n   870\t`14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:1476-1605`.\n   871\t\n   872\tThe construction scale exposes the square-root game error and\n   873\tSchwartz--Zippel loss that the source absorbs into `deltaS`.\n   874\t\n   875\t**Proof obligation:** issue #47 tracks the pulling-consistency calculation and\n   876\tthe final trace postprocessing from measurements to observables. -/\n   877\ttheorem tildeObs_selfConsistent :\n   878\t    ∃ C : ℝ, 1 ≤ C ∧\n   879\t      ∀ (P : AdmissibleParams) (epsilon deltaG : ℝ),\n   880\t        0 ≤ epsilon → epsilon ≤ 1 → 0 ≤ deltaG →\n   881\t          ∀ (S : ProjectiveSetting P epsilon)\n   882\t            (w : GlobalPairWitness S deltaG) (W : PauliKind)\n   883\t            (j : Fin P.model.basisDim),\n   884\t            opDistSq (uniformDistribution (PauliRegister P))\n   885\t              (fun u => S.placeSide .alice (tildeObs w .alice W u j))\n   886\t              (fun u => S.placeSide .bob (tildeObs w .bob W u j))\n   887\t              S.psiHat ≤\n   888\t                deltaConstructPaulis C epsilon deltaG P.m P.d P.q := by\n   889\t  sorry\n   890\t\n   891\tend\n   892\t\n   893\tend MIPStarRE.QPBT\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a09e58-29ec-7233-b950-156ba1fc0951","create_time":1789363264.8010116}},{"type":"reasoning","id":"rs_0dc45167a280c00e016aa784453c3487d0944b9a84e1e9d514","summary":[],"encrypted_content":"gAAAAABqp4RJGYyPXSnZMNZTWkva5ETj6ipRciUYU1hg0_KqdOrfsmpavSsnp1BKniQ0JNEAVyestfnnRTI8mkZNRNlNn3HVFQR3EIjlveYnlfxnWnGTmgC_EcESid1wvpahOICbAfwp8uIMR_7_zygKIiDHE7ZCIr5M0fkHblZWkoj8JryY7wugfEwtEaUdGzxs5kH2NzGZEQ4OCswT5gJvF5V6rxy_J4kdqw6CNE09-30hANTQsrykxyrfepqJf_8nD_LqisnHUYsgoLry05qcWV-Wj1GWzTQ-VXzcjPp6LgouIoeOZsOgRS3b6iCTkdtuuODJ08oRh4D9A03ggpM2NbPv22MKTHng70-ermBbkVGW3UohfIqqIc5pdZPNF5YHgkLW-m9S3zO0AYOlH3_cJst50ykn6nnpZP1DjRUyl7R4nQvYhKYx1j_V5bhUnwf7YgPT-2nt4Dj4-X78-bBHN2cAYXgRlw79QYpL-kYiwMLojpRXmtSRxjImRhSDBHteZ4T2cZg67KI4u_A7AxeOCGZOznaXIhtA6cVxewJGGVFZcfuv55E7M_R4PGwMrzpxHnvSq5oyrm4MMBmnI52Rx3xKI-KKs8itDOHzySh1d1_uc13vn-AVfPYMBaZkCGMlIE2u4E72nvOUkJBvWibrxvZqUFWR0QKXsB0pudVr7CgfkO0VVlO_AaB0YLY4OCbn3edxJTWEk87VmFhOY8MSI5U0HHmylHlqrmoNhG-SS3y2wy-DoYapIRg8li5QGosV0VVCku_r071ra-vG-xFpZOzo_89RwXOf9-7Z5Fw3PkIQg23NT8OQlPQvSFj8sW4SPdIRU1cXXfnZndY1FQGiFpEPrJBwzhHi7kB7GmE6pfG9TH9Eq07f_XfaiK0keRFOYJ-Wt7nC0E1LXOSnWR3NCbI_EF85ktVZX3EySI2Oqep6R-NMMWwRcNVS