277\t\\begin{proof}\n  1278\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} establishes the existence of a strategy $\\strategy_\\ld = (\\hat{\\psi}, \\{\n  1279\t        \\hat{Q}^\\line_f \\} \\cup \\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_a \\} )$\n  1280\t        for the classical low-degree test $\\game^\\ld_\\ldparams$ for\n  1281\t        $\\ldparams = (q,2m + 2,d, 1)$, with value $1 -\n  1282\t        \\delta_{\\combine}$. This strategy is not necessarily projective because the measurement $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\line}_f\\}$ is not guaranteed to be; however, we may projectivize it by applying Naimark's theorem to this measurement. This preserves the consistency ($\\simeq$) relation obtained as the conclusion of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-13}. Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} applied to this projectivized strategy implies the\n  1283\t        existence of a POVM $\\{ \\hat{S}_g \\}$ with\n  1284\t        outcomes $g \\in \\ideg_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ that is\n  1285\t        $\\delta_\\ld$-self-consistent and consistent with the\n  1286\t        ``points'' measurements $\\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_a\\}$,\n  1287\t        where $\\delta_\\ld = a(md)^a(\\delta_\\combine^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for some universal constants $a > 1, 0 < b < 1$ is given\n  1288\t        by Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. We may apply Naimark's theorem once again to $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ to obtain a projective measurement with the same consistency guarantees, and henceforth, we will use $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ to refer to this projective measurement.\n  1289\t\t\n  1290\t\tWe now argue that with high probability, the polynomial $g$ returned by the measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_g \\}$ has the form $g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) = \\alpha g_\\xpt(x) + \\beta g_\\zpt (z)$ for polynomials $g_\\xpt, g_\\zpt \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. Let $\\mathcal{G}$ denote the set of such polynomials. On average over uniformly random $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$, \n  1291\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1292\t          (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} &= (\\hat{S}_g)^2_{\\alice \\alice'} \\notag \\\\\n  1293\t                                       &\\approx_{\\delta_\\ld} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)})_{\\bob \\alice''}\\notag\\\\\n  1294\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_Q} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} (\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)})_{\\alice \\alice'}\\notag \\\\\n  1295\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_Q} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Big ( \\sum_{b,b'} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} \\, \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{b} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\Big)_{\\bob \\alice''} \\label{eq:qld-g-42} \\\\\n  1296\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_Q} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Big ( \\sum_{b,b'} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} \\, \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{b} \\Big)_{\\bob \\alice''} \\;.\\label{eq:qld-g-43} \t\t\n  1297\t\t\\end{align}\n  1298\t\t Here the first approximation uses the projectivity of $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$, and the first approximation uses the consistency of $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ with  $\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_a\\}$ from Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, together with Fact~\\ref{fact:agreement} to switch from $\\simeq$ to $\\approx$, and Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj2}. The second, third and fourth approximations follow from the consistency properties of $\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_a\\}$ from Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-12} combined with Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj2}. Note that we will need both \\Cref{eq:qld-g-42} and \\Cref{eq:qld-g-43} for the subsequent calculations.\n  1299\t\t\n  1300\t\tFor all $g$ define $ \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}_g} =(\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$. \n  1301\t\tThen on average over uniformly random $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$, \n  1302\t\t  \\begin{align}\n  1303\t\t  \t&\\E_{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta} \\norm{ \\Big ( \\sum_{b,b'} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} \\, \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{b} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\Big)_{\\bob \\alice''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}_g} }^2\\notag \\\\\n  1304\t\t\t=_{\\phantom{1/q}}& \\E_{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta} \\sum_{b,b',b''} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b'' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}_g}  \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b''} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{b} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}_g} \\notag\\\\\n  1305\t\t\t\\approx_{1/q}& \\E_{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta} \\sum_{b,b'} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}_g}  \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{b} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}_g}\n  1510\t\n  1511\t\\begin{remark}[Property~3 of the sub-line distribution]\n  1512\t\t\\label{rem:qld-sublines-property-three}\n  1513\t\tThe source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}\n  1514\t\tappeals to a ``Property~3'' of the sub-line lemma, while the statement\n  1515\t\tgiven there lists only two numbered properties, so the reference is\n  1516\t\tdangling. The intended assertion is the axis-parallel closure property\n  1517\t\trecorded as Property~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} above, which is\n  1518\t\timmediate from the sampling procedure in the proof.\n  1519\t\\end{remark}\n  1520\t\n  1521\tThe proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} rests on three scalar estimates, stated\n  1522\tnext. In all three, $D$ is the distribution\n  1523\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines},\n  1524\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}$ is the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} placed\n  1525\ton registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$, the second tensor factor is placed on\n  1526\tregisters $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, and the sums range over\n  1527\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$.\n  1528\t\n  1529\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support: the ordered product on one placement]\n  1530\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-10-same-placement}\n  1531\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.Placement.exists_isOpposite}\n  1532\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.CombinedPointsWitness.orderedZX_dist_le}\n  1533\t\t\\leanok\n  1534\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, fact:triangle, def:povm-distance}\n  1535\t\tOn one placement, the joint point measurement and the ordered\n  1536\t\t$Z$-then-$X$ product have squared distance at most $4\\delta_Q$.\n  1537\t\\end{lemma}\n  1538\t\\begin{proof}\n  1539\t\t\\leanok\n  1540\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, fact:triangle, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n  1541\t\tInsert the joint measurement on the opposite placement and apply the\n  1542\t\tsquared-distance triangle inequality to the two $\\delta_Q$ bounds.\n  1543\t\\end{proof}\n  1544\t\n  1545\t\\begin{lemma}[Formalization support: replacing one factor of a real overlap]\n  1546\t\t\\label{lem:overlap-gap-distance}\n  1547\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.abs_overlap_gap_le_sqrt_of_opFamilyDistSq}\n  1548\t\t\\leanok\n  1549\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:expanded-state}\n  1550\t\tA complete measurement weights the real part of the overlap gap between operator families by\n  1551\t\tat most the square root of their state-dependent squared distance.\n  1552\t\\end{lemma}\n  1553\t\\begin{proof}\n  1554\t\t\\leanok\n  1555\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:expanded-state, def:povm-conventions}\n  1556\t\tApply Cauchy--Schwarz to the outcome sum and then to the question average;\n  1557\t\tcompleteness bounds the squared norm of the left factor by one.\n  1558\t\\end{proof}\n  1559\t\n  1560\t\\begin{definition}[Directly indexed sub-line laws]\n  1561\t\t\\label{def:direct-subline-law}\n  1562\t\t\\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.SubLineWitness}\n  1563\t\t\\leanok\n  1564\t\t\\uses{def:ith-restricted-line, def:line-representative, def:combine-map}\n  1565\t\tPut $n=2m+2$. The directly indexed extended line-point law samples\n  1566\t\t$i\\in\\{0,\\dots,n-1\\}$ and $u,v\\in\\F_q^n$ independently and uniformly,\n  1567\t\tand chooses the axis or diagonal kind with equal probabilities.\n  1568\t\tFor the axis kind set $w=e_i$; for the diagonal kind set $w_j=0$ for\n  1569\t\t$j<i$ and $w_j=v_j$ for $j\\ge i$. The line has base $L_w^{\\mathrm{lnf}}(u)$\n  1570\t\tand direction $w$, retaining its kind and coordinate index; the sampled\n  1571\t\tpoint is $u$. Zero diagonal directions are included.\n  1572\t\n  1573\t\tA directly indexed sub-line law $D_{\\mathrm{dir}}$ is a probability law\n  1574\t\ton triples $(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z)$ with the following properties.\n  1575\t\tIts extended-line marginal is the line marginal just defined.\n  1576\t\tEvery supported triple admits affine maps $t\\mapsto a_W+b_Wt$ such that\n  1577\t\t\\[\n  1578\t\t\t\\pi_W(\\ell(t))=\\ell_W(a_W+b_Wt),\\qquad W\\in\\{X,Z\\},\n  1579\t\t\\]\n  1580\t\twhere $\\ell(t)$ denotes base plus parameter times direction and the\n  1581\t\tfield identifications are understood. Thus each extended point projects\n  1582\t\tonto its two source lines, with affine scalar coordinates $\\alpha(t)$\n  1583\t\tand $\\beta(t)$ as in Definition~\\ref{def:combine-map}. An axis-parallel\n  1584\t\textended line has axis-parallel source lines.\n  1585\t\n  1586\t\tFinally there is one probability law on\n  1587\t\t$(\\mathrm v,i,j)\\in\\{\\mathrm{ALine},\\mathrm{DLine}\\}\n  1588\t\t\\times\\{0,\\dots,m-1\\}^2$ with the following two mixture properties.\n  1589\t\tSample a triple from $D_{\\mathrm{dir}}$ and an independent uniform\n  1590\t\tparameter $t$ on $\\ell$. The law of $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,\\pi_X(\\ell(t)))$\n  1591\t\tis the mixture obtained by sampling $(\\ell_X,x)$ from\n  1592\t\t$D_{\\mathrm v,i}$ and independently $\\ell_Z$ from the line marginal of\n  1593\t\t$D_{\\mathrm v,j}$, and retaining $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x)$.\n  1594\t\tThe law of $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,\\pi_Z(\\ell(t)))$ is the mixture obtained\n  1595\t\tby retaining $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z)$ from the corresponding independent\n  1596\t\tsamples of $\\ell_X$ and $(\\ell_Z,z)$. These are separate one-point\n  1597\t\tmarginal identities, with the same mixture weights; they impose no\n  1598\t\tjoint conditional inde